#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499618} :activity/id #uuid "6b84de71-8564-517b-bc1e-eaec4c35e318" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049113 :topic/id #uuid "dba97a8b-19ad-5a52-bd24-261e0b206661" :topic/math-academy-id 1968} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08231533-7a2b-53e3-bb3b-f126a0945f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321209} {:answer/id #uuid "0b24974a-4a95-5964-a796-dfc9de32a894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321240} {:answer/id #uuid "3092878c-f858-5dc5-bfd5-49f9775ffbc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321519} {:answer/id #uuid "81980f53-11b8-52ef-bde5-882f1c8ff79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322168} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e1dfdd-2680-5cf5-8e11-29ac4f3cc003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322847}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321240} :answer-field/id #uuid "94a63c75-dcf0-5bbc-84eb-ef1e705e4ed9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e30ae3bf-a95b-5038-8bdd-b265809e9d12" :question/math-academy-id "q-37629" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{aligned}]$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )(-9-\\lambda )-(5)(-10) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0bea5b6f-6c05-5fe2-ad23-ee7c6d0550fe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "188ff12c-cc1a-5287-b72f-b735762cd4d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345568} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4889c1-8a99-5c50-8d21-7e1f3fa8f0f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346569} {:answer/id #uuid "97a5840f-59d5-5c8b-8a78-3953a0ad08a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346680} {:answer/id #uuid "a9bae287-d269-54df-b55a-ae11d68c915d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346833} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d5cbcb-c866-50ca-b5fd-06a7526077af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345568} :answer-field/id #uuid "00112189-18a5-5932-8481-85e29798e6dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4fad8010-b1d7-51d3-ae7c-0e9c4f636170" :question/math-academy-id "q-80453" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-(-2)I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."} :step/id #uuid "13e8d76b-e707-545b-91b0-3646c86cd6fd"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "218d2232-b6b4-5353-9b44-bc2b615b173f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345651} {:answer/id #uuid "54242d0d-9f70-50fb-a1c7-1d7500836483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346065} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d2abcd-e82b-5bca-8c24-21c2c07d708d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346799} {:answer/id #uuid "a6178168-bb39-5a85-9081-d612835e2505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346802} {:answer/id #uuid "ff40704f-1d0b-5ea5-8bf6-a3b2c43d0da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345651} :answer-field/id #uuid "e355db22-060d-55f4-890a-5652cc9577f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "def1c542-80f5-5b03-88dc-ad7a755f7e8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80371" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $K$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}b & 1 \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}b & 1 \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "66d23587-6ec7-592c-a133-1458d9c3ea42" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499595}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "639780ae-1398-50d7-b815-e2a195a0e73d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404102} {:answer/id #uuid "c73ad559-0eae-5f15-bdf7-739e613b69ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404103} {:answer/id #uuid "d0038efe-a96a-5281-ad36-c4af7f9c03a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404104} {:answer/id #uuid "c0406221-3155-5859-8236-e58b9f481700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404105} {:answer/id #uuid "d939f8e6-1f46-548c-8adc-229c62fd7d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=1,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404102} :answer-field/id #uuid "b352245b-eaa4-5fde-aea9-5afac2d00b93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "84388249-87e9-543c-a81f-fd99783cee24" :question/math-academy-id "q-80392" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $B$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}b & -3 \\\\ c & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}b & -3 \\\\ c & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "6bf05bcb-22b4-547e-acbe-f43cad6ac0ce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499573}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d029107-e148-55f6-a354-f22baa574250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321621} {:answer/id #uuid "56d1c400-959f-56c2-965a-3832f8ef8340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321833} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf1e1f5-79f6-557b-a763-a51889feac40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322613} {:answer/id #uuid "c23b64d9-c049-5e10-af78-713101e3ae50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322654} {:answer/id #uuid "db767106-a9b0-5dd4-8b49-d91b65e0543f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322867}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322654} :answer-field/id #uuid "79947e83-1100-52b3-a7fb-76ec24310ce3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "334a7a7c-883e-54c4-9c3a-6e918b1922ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-37107" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & -1 \\\\ b & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of  $\\frac{b}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nSo, the  first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "c44bbf6a-6f50-5fd9-883e-0b5cb944bf04" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499572}}] :activity/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498723}]}
